    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c1609b60_86f62e22",
        "filename": "src/gpu/graphite/render/VerticesRenderStep.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 152,
      "author": {
        "id": 18914
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-08-17T17:22:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Update: using simply devPoints[0] and t[0] compiled OK, but led to an error at runtime within BufferWriter::validate at this assert: `SkASSERT(!fEnd || Mark(fPtr, bytesToWrite) \u003c\u003d fEnd);`\n\nSo I now do devPoints[0].x \u003c\u003c devPoints[0].y \u003c\u003c ..., which works fine and appears to be how we do it in other RenderSteps. Might be worth looking into though if this is unexpected behavior.",
      "parentUuid": "e7f53754_799c15b8",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 152,
        "startChar": 27,
        "endLine": 152,
        "endChar": 66
      },
      "revId": "40766716f5fc11f434563d5cbc8c98ccbbbd5ef0",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "10de71b0_a5bc72cd",
        "filename": "src/gpu/graphite/render/VerticesRenderStep.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 152,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-08-17T20:01:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "That is surprising to me. That happens if too many bytes are written, which would happen if we had a mismatch between our vertex attribute definitions and the types we were sending to the vertex writer.\n\nI didn\u0027t see any obvious mismatch, so I wonder if the vertex writer \u003c\u003c template is getting confused by `t` itself being an array of 3 SkV2\u0027s and tries to advance that many instead of seeing it as `t[0]` and just needing two floats. \n\nSince we aren\u0027t transforming the coords, can you try just passing in `texCoords[state.f0]` as an SkPoint. That would be both simpler than seeing the x/y accesses and skips the copy into the `t` variable.\n\nAfter this lands, I think it\u0027ll be worthwhile trying to reproduce the runtime assertions to see if we can understand what was going on when we were writing SkV2s from an array.",
      "parentUuid": "c1609b60_86f62e22",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 152,
        "startChar": 27,
        "endLine": 152,
        "endChar": 66
      },
      "revId": "40766716f5fc11f434563d5cbc8c98ccbbbd5ef0",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3a64f8d3_ba68e14e",
        "filename": "src/gpu/graphite/render/VerticesRenderStep.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 152,
      "author": {
        "id": 18914
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-08-17T20:42:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Okay, after some more experimentation:\n`t[0]` works and `texCoords[state.f0]` also works. \n\n`devPoints[0]` was the culprit which requires the x and y accesses. I think this makes sense because `devPosition` is an array of `SkV4`s (has 4 floats), whereas `position` in this class is `kFloat2`. This was modeled after some other render steps (such as `BitmapTextRenderStep`,`SDFTextRenderStep`) and is helpful for assigning directly to `texCoords` which is also `kFloat2` type (since texture coordinates are passed in as one 2D point).\n\nI propose we do the following: \n`devPoints[0].x \u003c\u003c devPoints[0].y \u003c\u003c texCoords[state.f0]...`\nThis will get rid of `t` and doesn\u0027t require making `position` a `float4` type, which would inconvenience the assignment to `texCoords`. Let me know what you think.",
      "parentUuid": "10de71b0_a5bc72cd",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 152,
        "startChar": 27,
        "endLine": 152,
        "endChar": 66
      },
      "revId": "40766716f5fc11f434563d5cbc8c98ccbbbd5ef0",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f07b2245_6ee7d6ea",
        "filename": "src/gpu/graphite/render/VerticesRenderStep.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 153,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-08-16T17:10:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t know if it\u0027s worth doing for performance in this case, but there is a special \"type\" called `VertexWriter::If(bool, value)` that would let you do something like `\u003c\u003c If(fHasTexture, t[0])` in the regular writeVertices(). Then if fHasTexture is false, the texture coord won\u0027t be evaluated.\n\nBut using If has a little bit of overhead, and since there\u0027s often a lot of vertices that could add up, so it\u0027s also fine to have these split up so the branch happens just once at the start.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 153,
        "startChar": 27,
        "endLine": 153,
        "endChar": 50
      },
      "revId": "40766716f5fc11f434563d5cbc8c98ccbbbd5ef0",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "da229916_3819c799",
        "filename": "src/gpu/graphite/render/VerticesRenderStep.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 153,
      "author": {
        "id": 18914
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-08-17T14:12:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yeah, that\u0027s a good option to consider and to have in the tool belt. I do think that overhead would add up fairly quickly in this case though, especially once we introduce color as well.",
      "parentUuid": "f07b2245_6ee7d6ea",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 153,
        "startChar": 27,
        "endLine": 153,
        "endChar": 50
      },
      "revId": "40766716f5fc11f434563d5cbc8c98ccbbbd5ef0",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
